Thank you again for your detailed and insightful review. We now recognize that your evaluation is framed around whether the paper provides a methodological contribution to self-supervised learning (SSL). Your comments helped us understand that our framing may have unintentionally suggested a broader SSL contribution than intended. We appreciate the opportunity to clarify and to revise the manuscript so that its scientific contribution is communicated more clearly.

1\. On alternative SSL paradigms  
We thank the reviewer for the seasoned and technically informed suggestions regarding generative, contrastive, and masked paradigms. These remarks help situate our work within the broader SSL landscape, and we appreciate the opportunity to refine this part of the paper.  
While these directions are valuable, our evaluation follows established practice in large Earth foundation models such as Prithvi, Terramind, AlphaEarth, and Aurora (Bodnar et al., Nature, 2025), which also employ a single SSL paradigm. This is due in part to the massive data volumes and the combinatorial space of pretraining strategies, adapters, finetuning configurations, and dataset choices. These models likewise do not conduct full cross-paradigm comparisons, and our study aligns with this evaluation norm.  
Furthermore, existing SSL approaches for tabular or time-series data (SCARF, SubTab, TS-TCC, TabBERT, UniTTab) operate under assumptions that do not directly accommodate heterogeneous, multi-source geospatial data:  
	•	features are treated as homogeneous;  
	•	static and dynamic inputs are not jointly modeled;  
	•	masking is typically uniform across timesteps or variables.  
Our model instead supports variable-specific and time-specific random masking, which enables patterns such as masking only selected variables over selected temporal segments—functionality not present in standard MAE/tabular SSL frameworks.  
We will expand our discussion to contrast our design choices with these paradigms and highlight how their principles might inspire future tailored SSL approaches for geoscience. For example, we will include the following:  
“While SSL paradigms such as SCARF, SubTab, TS-TCC, TabBERT, and UniTTab are promising, their current formulations assume homogeneous features and uniform masking and therefore do not support the variable-wise and time-wise random masking required for heterogeneous geospatial inputs. Their underlying ideas may, however, inspire future extensions tailored to land-surface systems.”

2\. Nature of the contribution  
We do not claim to introduce a new SSL paradigm. Rather, the main contribution is scientific and insight-focused, consistent with AI4Science objectives:  
	•	demonstrating that curated scalar attributes can efficiently encode cross-domain process interactions, providing a lightweight, interpretable alternative to raw imagery for downstream land-surface tasks;  
	•	demonstrating that curated multi-source scalar attributes+time series MAE can form an effective pretraining channel;  
	•	showing that these domain-informed attributes measurably improve spatial generalization, a long-standing challenge in hydrology and land-surface modeling;  
	•	evaluating against several geoscience foundation models (Prithvi, Terramind, AlphaEarth) that use very different pretraining strategies and much larger data volumes;  
	•	and demonstrating applicability across hydrology, soil applications, and landslides, spanning multiple major geoscience subfields with large communities.  
These findings relate to the scientific structure of land-surface systems and may also be relevant for domains where meaningful scalar and temporal attributes (rather than raw imagery) are available.  
We will revise the introduction and contribution statement to foreground these scientific questions more explicitly, as the reviewer suggested.

3\. Domain-specific architectural adaptations  
Although our approach draws on MAE concepts, several elements were designed specifically for geoscience:  
	•	Variable- and time-specific masking: different variables can be masked across different temporal segments, e.g., masking rainfall while unmask temperature on the same day, enabling the model to learn cross-variable temporal interactions;  
	•	Residual adapter enables static spatial attributes to fused with the LSTM temporal pathway, a design validated in ablations;  
	•	Coupled static–dynamic training objective: reflecting that land-surface processes are driven jointly by landscape characteristics and external forcings.  
We agree that these points should be described more clearly in the paper, and we will revise Section 3 accordingly.

4\. Reframing the expected contribution  
We appreciate the reviewer’s emphasis that strong AI4Science work should integrate scientific insight with appropriate machine-learning methodology. With this framing:  
	•	we do not claim MAE is uniquely optimal;  
	•	we do not propose a new SSL paradigm;  
	•	instead, the work advances understanding of how static–dynamic interactions govern spatial generalization;  
	•	and provides a tailored SSL configuration that operationalizes this insight.  
Your feedback has helped us significantly improve how we communicate this distinction. We will update the manuscript to ensure the scientific scope and methodological choices are presented with much greater clarity.  
This work represents a broadly applicable contribution within geoscience AI, demonstrated across four diverse land-surface tasks. We sincerely appreciate the reviewer’s rigorous and thoughtful feedback, which has strengthened the paper. We hope that the clarified framing will help evaluate the work within its intended scientific context.

Bodnar et al., Nature, A foundation model for the Earth system, doi: 10.1038/s41586-025-09005-y
